Here is a breakdown of how to handle your 2024 taxes based on the information provided. 

***Disclaimer:** I am an AI, not a CPA or tax professional. This is general educational information. You should consult a qualified tax professional or use certified tax software to file your return.*

### 1. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing
**Recommendation: Take the Standard Deduction.**
*   For 2024, the Standard Deduction for Married Filing Jointly (MFJ) is **$29,200**.
*   Your itemizable expenses include your $2,000 charitable donation to St. Mary's Food Bank and your State and Local Taxes (SALT). Even with California state taxes, your total itemized deductions will likely fall far short of the $29,200 standard deduction. 
*   *Note on Unreimbursed Expenses ($780):* If these are related to your W-2 job at Riverside Hospital, they are **no longer deductible** on your federal return (the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act suspended W-2 employee expense deductions through 2025). If they are related to your graphic design business, you can deduct them directly against your business income (see below).

### 2. Above-the-Line Deductions (Adjustments to Income)
Even if you take the standard deduction, you can still claim "above-the-line" deductions:
*   **Student Loan Interest:** The IRS caps the student loan interest deduction at **$2,500** per year. You can deduct $2,500 of the $2,700 you paid. Based on your combined income (~$92k), you are well below the phase-out limit for MFJ, so you should qualify for the full $2,500 deduction.

### 3. Freelance Income & Home Office Deduction
Your $11,200 from graphic design is considered self-employment income. You can deduct business expenses against this income, including your home office.
*   **Home Office Percentage:** 165 sq ft / 690 sq ft = **~23.9%** of your home is used exclusively for business.
*   **Actual Expenses Method:** You pay $28,800/year in rent ($2,400 x 12). 23.9% of your rent is **~$6,886**. You can also deduct 23.9% of your renter's insurance, utilities, and internet.
*   **Simplified Method:** 165 sq ft x $5/sq ft = **$825**.
*   *Verdict:* The **Actual Expenses method** will give you a significantly larger deduction, drastically reducing the taxable profit of your freelance business.

### 4. Forms You Will Need
When filing your federal return, you will need the following forms and schedules:
*   **Form 1040:** The main tax return document.
*   **Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income):** To report your graphic design business income and claim your $2,500 student loan interest deduction.
*   **Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends):** Because your combined interest and dividends ($1,780 + $195 = $1,975) exceed the $1,500 threshold, you must itemize the sources on this schedule.
*   **Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business):** To report your $11,200 graphic design income and deduct any direct business expenses (like software, supplies, or the $780 in unreimbursed expenses if they were for your freelance work).
*   **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** To calculate and claim your home office deduction using the actual expenses method.
*   **Schedule 2 & Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax):** To calculate the Medicare and Social Security taxes owed on your net freelance profit. 

*Note for California:* California tax law sometimes differs from federal law (for example, CA still allows W-2 unreimbursed employee expenses if they exceed 2% of your AGI). Tax software will automatically handle these state-level differences for you.

